INT. MATTRESS SHOWROOM - DAY

A cavernous warehouse of beds. Banners scream "BLOWOUT." A fake sunrise glows over a king-size.

DUANE PETTIGREW, forties, a salesman in a shiny tie, hovers over a couple. LUCINDA FROST, thirties, bounces gently on a firm model. Her husband THEO FROST, thirties, lies flat on a plush one with his shoes on.

DUANE
This one has four layers of memory foam.

LUCINDA
I'll take your word.

Duane clasps his hands. Theo rolls over and sinks into the foam with a long sigh while Lucinda stands and moves to a third bed, which Duane hurriedly pats, and while Theo's shoes drag a smear of mud across the quilted top, Duane gasps and lunges with a wipe, and Lucinda kneels to inspect the springs.

THEO
I can't get up.

LUCINDA
It's only a mattress.

THEO
It has me.

DUANE
That's the contouring technology!

Duane takes Theo's arm and heaves. Theo comes up an inch and falls back. Lucinda grabs his other arm. They pull in unison and the three tumble off the edge of the bed in a heap, the sign "BLOWOUT" swaying overhead.
